WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.000
On September 4, 2020,

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:03.440
the Centers for Disease Control

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.960
halted residential evictions
in the United States

00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:07.390
for nonpayment of rent,

00:00:07.390 --> 00:00:09.570
because of the COVID19 pandemic.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:11.200
Eviction protection still needs

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.880
to be the very top priority.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:15.590
Forcing thousands of
people out of their homes

00:00:15.590 --> 00:00:20.430
during a pandemic will make
a public health crisis worse.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.050
Many states and municipalities
got there first,

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:25.280
imposing at least partial
eviction moratorium

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.810
starting in March and April.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.750
The theory behind these emergency orders

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:31.230
was that if Americans were
forced to leave their homes

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.320
and move into more crowded settings,

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.890
it would increase transmission
of the coronavirus.

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:39.070
The federal ban was supposed
to expire at the end of 2020.

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:41.360
Then it was extended a
month, then two more months,

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.430
then three more months

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:44.120
and then until the end of July.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.190
Many states and cities

00:00:45.190 --> 00:00:47.090
have also extended their moratoriums,

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.200
in some cases through
the end of September,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.960
even as COVID infection and
death rates are plummeting.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:54.200
That's gonna make matters
even worse for landlords.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.380
The smaller mom and pop landlords

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.030
who depend the most on rent
for their personal income

00:00:59.030 --> 00:01:01.240
are being hardest hit
by renters not paying.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.830
Tenants will end up picking up the tab.

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:03.663
I pay it.

00:01:03.663 --> 00:01:06.050
I have to raise rents to cover
the eight or nine tenants

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.460
who aren't paying that I have.

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:09.490
Small landlord, who make up the majority

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.390
of rental property owners in America

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:12.890
have been forced to raise rents,

00:01:12.890 --> 00:01:14.450
remove units from the housing market,

00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:17.100
or refuse law income or
credit impaired tenants.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:19.586
Fair laws for landlords.

00:01:19.586 --> 00:01:20.970
Were the eviction bans necessary

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.550
to protect public health
during the pandemic?

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.880
Two studies that got
widespread media attention,

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:28.130
and that have also been cited
by the Federal Government

00:01:28.130 --> 00:01:29.540
to support its policies,

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:32.700
claim to show that the moratoriums
saved thousands of lives.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:34.820
These studies that have
been widely publicized

00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.430
are seriously flawed.

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:40.300
The underlying data
inconsistent and incomplete.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.770
The results are implausible.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:44.520
The size of the effect is
wildly disproportionate

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.830
to other public health interventions.

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:50.280
And the researchers claim an
absurd amount of certainty

00:01:50.280 --> 00:01:52.130
despite the fact that the underlying data

00:01:52.130 --> 00:01:53.300
are highly uncertain.

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.890
Aaron Brown is a statistician

00:01:54.890 --> 00:01:57.310
who has taught at NYU and UCSD.

00:01:57.310 --> 00:01:59.230
He's also the former Chief Risk Officer

00:01:59.230 --> 00:02:01.720
at the hedge fund, AQR Capital Management.

00:02:01.720 --> 00:02:03.060
If these studies are correct,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.860
they should be in big, bold headlines

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:06.610
of every newspaper in America.

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:09.180
They would be the most
dramatic public health finding

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:10.130
in history.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:12.720
One study from Duke University researchers

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:14.210
found that compared to a scenario

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:16.260
without any eviction moratoriums,

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:17.770
policies that limit evictions

00:02:17.770 --> 00:02:20.700
could have reduced COVID19
infections by 14.2%

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.640
and deaths by 40.7%.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:26.280
The second study, whose lead author

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.620
is a postdoctoral researcher at UCLA,

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:30.530
found that in states
that had eviction bans

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:33.620
but lifted them before the
CDC moratorium kicked in,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:38.620
there were between 8,988
and 12,470 excess deaths

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.940
caused by lifting the moratoriums.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.390
To have the CDC come out

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.830
in the context of the pandemic

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.640
and say that this was their rationale

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.660
for the moratorium was really powerful.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.790
Brown says the first
problem with these studies

00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.740
is that they draw strong
conclusions from poor data.

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.290
Information on where, when,
and how many evictions occur

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.280
in the US is collected by a patchwork

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:00.010
of local governments and courts,

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:02.440
and is notoriously
inaccurate and incomplete.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:05.140
The important question
for public policy is,

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.460
if we have eviction moratorium,

00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:09.650
will it reduce COVID deaths?

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:12.090
In order to establish a
causal link of that sort,

00:03:12.090 --> 00:03:14.440
you'd gotta work through it step by step.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:17.650
How do the eviction
moratorium affect evictions?

00:03:17.650 --> 00:03:19.350
How do evictions affected crowding?

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.800
How does crowding affect
virus transmission?

00:03:21.800 --> 00:03:26.080
How does virus transmission
affect infections and deaths?

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.770
The researchers don't even try to do this.

00:03:28.770 --> 00:03:31.103
And they can't because
we don't have the data

00:03:31.103 --> 00:03:32.940
in each of those links.

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.750
Brown says the studies' final numbers

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.240
are particularly implausible

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.040
when compared to other
public health interventions.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.490
The Duke study, for example,

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.610
claims that had the
federal eviction moratorium

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.330
been in place between
March and November of 2020,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:48.500
about 40 percent of all
Americans who died from COVID19

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:50.320
in that period would have been spared.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:54.280
In other words, about 122,000
lives would have been saved

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.810
compared to a scenario
with no moratoriums at all.

00:03:56.810 --> 00:03:58.290
To put that into context,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.170
the CDC estimates that 600,000 Americans

00:04:01.170 --> 00:04:03.450
have died from COVID19 in total.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.310
The moratoriums

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.500
might have prevented tens
of thousands of evictions.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.660
Even if we ignore the offsetting effect

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:12.170
of people moving into the vacated housing

00:04:12.170 --> 00:04:14.450
and reducing crowding,
the eviction moratorium

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:16.450
might have reduce the COVID infection

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:17.930
by a few hundred people.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.680
Even allowing for a super spreader event,

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.550
it's unlikely that the
evictees would have caused

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:25.790
more than a few thousand
additional infection

00:04:25.790 --> 00:04:27.060
in the population.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.880
Using the casemortality
ratios at the time,

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.530
gives us a number of
approximately a hundred deaths

00:04:32.530 --> 00:04:35.550
that might have been prevented
by eviction moratorium.

00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:37.950
But the researchers claim high confidence

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.950
for thousands or tens of
thousands of lives save,

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.520
that's just implausible.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.170
Statistical studies not
only present findings,

00:04:45.170 --> 00:04:47.640
they also include what's
known as confidence intervals

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.220
to express the level of certainty

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.800
we should have in the results.

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:53.850
In both studies, the
authors' claim 95% confidence

00:04:53.850 --> 00:04:55.330
in their final conclusions.

00:04:55.330 --> 00:04:57.420
The most precise data both studies use

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:58.990
is the number of COVID deaths.

00:04:58.990 --> 00:05:01.000
And serious researchers
disagree about that

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.890
by a factor of 60%.

00:05:02.890 --> 00:05:05.160
The studies rely on long chains of data,

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:09.280
each link of which has much
greater uncertainty than 60%,

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.510
And the final uncertainty,

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.160
their answer has to be the product

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.260
of all of the individual uncertainties.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:15.490
Yet, these research claim

00:05:15.490 --> 00:05:19.510
they can evaluate the causal
effect of eviction moratorium

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:22.650
with standard errors
of roughly 10% or 20%.

00:05:22.650 --> 00:05:25.223
And they have 95% confidence
in those estimates.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.380
Angela] Admitting county's band evictions,

00:05:28.380 --> 00:05:30.360
now researchers from
Duke say those places,

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:33.440
they're seeing fewer cases
and deaths due to COVID19.

00:05:33.440 --> 00:05:34.930
The Duke study was coauthored

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.760
by a team of academics with
prestigious credentials,

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:41.010
including a professor of economics
with a PhD from Stanford,

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.950
and a quantitative epidemiologist

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:44.940
and assistant professor of Medicine.

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:46.290
In the study, which was published

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:48.300
in the National Bureau
of Economic Researchers'

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:49.710
Working Paper series,

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.490
the authors didn't include the datasets

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:53.330
they had used to reach their conclusions.

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.630
And listed just two of
their sources of data.

00:05:55.630 --> 00:05:57.440
So, Reason contacted the authors

00:05:57.440 --> 00:05:58.930
and asked to see their math.

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:01.110
As the US Department of
Health and Human Services

00:06:01.110 --> 00:06:03.137
Office of Research Integrity states,

00:06:03.137 --> 00:06:04.610
"Once a researcher has published

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:05.960
the results of an experiment,

00:06:05.960 --> 00:06:08.210
it is generally expected
that all the information

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.530
about that experiment,
including the final data,

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:12.720
should be freely available
for other researchers

00:06:12.720 --> 00:06:13.980
to check and use."

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:16.410
These guidelines apply to
federally funded studies,

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.430
but are standard practice
among academic researchers.

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.770
But Duke University denied our request

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.060
on the grounds that the study
hasn't been peer-reviewed,

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.540
and therefore wasn't
considered published work.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:28.770
The Duke paper is what's
known as a pre-print,

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.170
in which researchers make
a study publicly available

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.540
prior to submitting it
to an academic journal.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.290
And yet the authors gave interviews

00:06:35.290 --> 00:06:37.020
in which they promoted their findings.

00:06:37.020 --> 00:06:39.270
And the paper was widely
cited in the press,

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:41.140
appears twice in the Federal Register

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:42.380
as part of rule-making issued

00:06:42.380 --> 00:06:44.530
by the Consumer Financial
Protection Bureau,

00:06:44.530 --> 00:06:46.270
and was cited in an amicus brief

00:06:46.270 --> 00:06:47.840
at the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:50.340
to support legal arguments
that eviction moratoriums

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.350
have spared COVID19 deaths.

00:06:52.350 --> 00:06:55.100
Duke also trumpeted the
findings in a press release

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:57.170
that encouraged members
of the media interested

00:06:57.170 --> 00:06:59.380
in speaking with the
authors to get in touch.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:00.213
Though apparently not

00:07:00.213 --> 00:07:02.300
if that meant scrutinizing
the actual dataset

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:04.860
or code used to generate
the paper's claims.

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:06.950
The Duke researchers only cite sources

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:08.800
for the two of the data elements they use

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.920
and those citations incomplete.

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.310
They claim to have gotten
county-level death data

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:15.700
from the COVID Tracking
Project, but that project

00:07:15.700 --> 00:07:17.750
doesn't have county level data at all.

00:07:17.750 --> 00:07:19.610
The Duke researchers also won't tell us

00:07:19.610 --> 00:07:21.020
when they accessed the data,

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:23.070
or how they matched it up with other data.

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:25.830
If someone says they ran
a multivariate regression,

00:07:25.830 --> 00:07:29.140
you wanna know the exact code
and adjustments they made.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.330
This the only way independent researchers

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:33.210
can validate the findings.

00:07:33.210 --> 00:07:34.310
Even without access

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:36.080
to the researchers' data or code,

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:38.320
Brown was able to
discern a variety of ways

00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:40.820
in which the study misuses
statistical techniques

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:42.830
to arrive at faulty conclusions.

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.760
First, the authors look only
at differences within states,

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.530
when one locality had an
eviction moratorium in place

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:49.640
and another didn't.

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.070
But they don't compare the
COVID rates in entire states

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:54.820
that had an eviction moratorium
with those that didn't.

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:56.830
Brown says it's clear
that at the state level,

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:58.810
those with moratoriums
actually experienced

00:07:58.810 --> 00:08:01.350
more COVID deaths than
states without them.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:02.610
The orange line shows states

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:05.110
that always had an eviction
moratorium in place.

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:06.620
The black line represents states

00:08:06.620 --> 00:08:08.240
that never banned evictions.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.240
This finding, like the Duke study,

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.130
is a correlation and almost
certainly not causal.

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:15.200
It doesn't prove that eviction moratoriums

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.330
cause death.

00:08:16.330 --> 00:08:18.770
What it does do is put the burden of proof

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.750
on anyone claiming a
dramatic health benefit

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.670
from eviction moratoriums to
explain why it doesn't show up

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:25.980
in the aggregate data.

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:28.540
In fact, why we see the opposite.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:31.530
The Duke study is arguing
that eviction moratoriums

00:08:31.530 --> 00:08:35.210
caused COVID deaths rate to
decline, and dramatically.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.700
If that were true, we should
see the effect at all levels,

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.820
both within the states and between states.

00:08:39.820 --> 00:08:41.430
But the latter data doesn't support

00:08:41.430 --> 00:08:44.200
the authors' conclusions,
so they ignored it.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:45.950
The authors also ignore the timing

00:08:45.950 --> 00:08:48.230
of when people died from COVID19.

00:08:48.230 --> 00:08:50.270
After states lifted their moratoriums,

00:08:50.270 --> 00:08:51.640
they had the same death rates

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.790
as states that kept their bans in place.

00:08:53.790 --> 00:08:55.380
And places that had moratoriums

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:58.790
saw a major increase in COVID
deaths in the summer of 2020.

00:08:58.790 --> 00:09:00.940
While in places that
never banned evictions,

00:09:00.940 --> 00:09:02.410
death rates remained low.

00:09:02.410 --> 00:09:04.840
The other big data
problems for the authors

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:06.870
is that COVID death rates were higher

00:09:06.870 --> 00:09:08.990
when eviction moratorium were in place

00:09:08.990 --> 00:09:10.200
than when they weren't.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:13.540
They don't make the obvious
comparison of one place

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:17.340
when it had an eviction
moratorium versus when it didn't.

00:09:17.340 --> 00:09:20.700
All they do is they compare
two different places,

00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:22.890
one of which had an eviction moratorium

00:09:22.890 --> 00:09:24.223
and one of which didn't.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.540
These two statistical manipulations

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:28.850
allow the authors to get rid

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:30.970
of the obvious objections to their study.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:34.380
All we can say is we have
no idea from the data

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.603
whether there's any causal
effect, one way or the other.

00:09:37.603 --> 00:09:40.090
That eviction moratorium
increase COVID death rates,

00:09:40.090 --> 00:09:42.370
decrease them, or have no affect at all.

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:43.210
We don't know.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.510
A new UCLA paper out this week,

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:49.130
directly links evictions
to the spread of COVID19.

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:50.790
The study is awaiting peer review

00:09:50.790 --> 00:09:53.410
but we wanted to make
sure that policy makers

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:56.290
have these numbers in hand
as they're making decision.

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:57.760
Another widely cited study,

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.430
which also hasn't been peer-reviewed

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:01.270
or appeared in an academic journal,

00:10:01.270 --> 00:10:03.930
looks at a period in which
the CDC eviction moratorium

00:10:03.930 --> 00:10:06.860
wasn't in place, during which
evictions were partly allowed

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:08.430
in 27 states.

00:10:08.430 --> 00:10:13.140
It argues that there were
between 8,988 and 12,470

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.830
excess deaths caused by
evictions in those states

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:16.970
during this period.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:20.520
It also claims a 95% confidence
interval for that finding.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:21.353
This study,

00:10:21.353 --> 00:10:23.690
written by an interdisciplinary
team of researchers

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:25.900
from several premier
American universities,

00:10:25.900 --> 00:10:28.660
was cited by the CDC
in its March 31st order

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:30.930
extending the federal eviction moratorium.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:33.970
Like the Duke study, it was
cited in the Federal Register

00:10:33.970 --> 00:10:35.120
and in the amicus brief

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.080
to the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:38.430
The study's lead author,

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:40.630
postdoctoral researcher Kathryn Leifheit

00:10:40.630 --> 00:10:43.140
of the UCLA Fielding
School of Public Health,

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.780
promoted the paper's conclusions

00:10:44.780 --> 00:10:49.250
in interviews with NPR, Vox,
CNBC, ABC, and other outlets.

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:53.590
Whenever you see numbers
like 430,000 cases,

00:10:53.590 --> 00:10:56.960
10,000 deaths, these are
deaths that could have been

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:00.150
prevented had the states
maintained their moratoriums.

00:11:00.150 --> 00:11:03.460
It really is a human tragedy
and not just numbers.

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:04.720
But when Reason reached out to her

00:11:04.720 --> 00:11:06.400
for an interview and said we were working

00:11:06.400 --> 00:11:08.960
with a statistician to
examine the underlying data,

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:10.680
she didn't reply to our query.

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:12.630
This study is a model of transparent,

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:13.750
scientific reporting.

00:11:13.750 --> 00:11:15.900
The authors tells you exactly
where they got their data

00:11:15.900 --> 00:11:17.940
and exactly what they did with it.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.290
I can duplicate their study.

00:11:20.290 --> 00:11:23.190
That also means I can pinpoint
exactly what they did wrong.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:24.820
This study gets around the problem

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:26.520
of the shoddy data on evictions

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:29.180
by comparing death rates
at times when moratoriums

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.210
were in force versus times
when they were not in force.

00:11:32.210 --> 00:11:34.400
Like the Duke study,
it's looking to tease out

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:36.790
the effect of a policy
through correlation.

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:38.710
The researchers note in some places

00:11:38.710 --> 00:11:40.880
when eviction moratoriums were listed,

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:42.440
COVID death rates went up.

00:11:42.440 --> 00:11:44.630
But they don't tell you is in most places,

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:45.930
the opposite happened.

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:48.290
And in the aggregate,
the opposite happened.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:51.240
More than half the study's
total of extra deaths,

00:11:51.240 --> 00:11:53.940
they attribute to lifting
eviction moratoriums

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.880
come from Texas and South Carolina.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:11:57.800
If this were a genuine causal effect,

00:11:57.800 --> 00:11:59.450
you expect to see it in every state

00:11:59.450 --> 00:12:00.880
or at least in most states.

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:03.440
And you'd expect to see it
when the federal eviction ban

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:04.580
were put in place.

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:06.840
The fact that the authors
can find a strong effect

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:08.410
in only two out of 50 states,

00:12:08.410 --> 00:12:11.030
says that this cannot
possibly be a causal effect.

00:12:11.030 --> 00:12:13.280
Further complicating the study's results,

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:15.700
while it's true that in
South Carolina and Texas

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:17.860
there were no statewide
eviction moratoriums

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.080
during the study period,

00:12:19.080 --> 00:12:22.450
major cities and counties within
Texas had some type of ban.

00:12:22.450 --> 00:12:24.610
And in South Carolina,
the State Supreme Court

00:12:24.610 --> 00:12:27.370
ordered strong restrictions
on eviction statewide.

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:29.440
And nationwide, many jurisdictions

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:31.190
that didn't have explicit moratoriums

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:34.090
still effectively halted
evictions for months at a time

00:12:34.090 --> 00:12:37.150
as courts were shut down by
social distancing directives.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:39.690
Others layered on emergency
tenant protections,

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:42.820
subsidized housing costs, or
abated eviction proceedings

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:44.630
for a period of weeks to months.

00:12:44.630 --> 00:12:46.040
All of these places are counted

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.280
as having no eviction moratorium at all.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.270
When Brown adjusts the authors' analysis

00:12:50.270 --> 00:12:51.950
to account for local moratoriums

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:53.680
and effective bans on eviction.

00:12:53.680 --> 00:12:55.560
The authors' effect goes away.

00:12:55.560 --> 00:12:57.340
Whether or not they did so knowingly,

00:12:57.340 --> 00:12:59.750
Brown says the authors ended
up manipulating the data

00:12:59.750 --> 00:13:02.500
to produce results that
don't pass the sniff test.

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:03.690
The elephant in the room

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:05.250
is the last couple of decades

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:08.830
have seen floods of misconduct
including fraudulent data,

00:13:08.830 --> 00:13:12.410
no data, and radically
misrepresented data.

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:15.420
We see it at the top
institutions, the top researchers,

00:13:15.420 --> 00:13:16.970
top scientists.

00:13:16.970 --> 00:13:20.130
And the most discouraging part
is institutions and journals

00:13:20.130 --> 00:13:22.630
circle the wagons to protect wrongdoers

00:13:22.630 --> 00:13:25.180
and slander anyone who
questions the results.

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:27.520
Duke had one of the most expensive

00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:29.280
and prominent examples of this.

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:31.330
When over a decade, the University

00:13:31.330 --> 00:13:33.050
protected a medical researcher

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.030
publishing false medical data.

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.730
The University promised
this would not happen again.

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:39.260
But the next chance they got,

00:13:39.260 --> 00:13:40.690
when researchers were promoting

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:42.640
dramatic public health claims

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:44.450
but refusing to release the data,

00:13:44.450 --> 00:13:46.200
the University closed ranks.

00:13:46.200 --> 00:13:48.730
While at the same time,
pushing the findings out

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:51.650
to any gullible reporters or
policymakers they can find

00:13:51.650 --> 00:13:53.130
The document Kathryn Leifheit

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.920
has compiled is deaths.

00:13:54.920 --> 00:13:58.110
There are states, percentages,
graphs, and numbers.

00:13:58.110 --> 00:13:59.260
Lots of numbers.

00:13:59.260 --> 00:14:01.390
The study is still in pre-print.

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:04.200
That means it has not
been peer-reviewed yet.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:06.400
Normally, we wouldn't
cover a study like that.

00:14:06.400 --> 00:14:07.830
Thanks to journalist like you,

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:10.371
it is getting a fair amount of attention.

00:14:10.371 --> 00:14:11.750
Cancel the rent.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:13.950
Tell that landlord he don't get a cent.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:15.790
"Cancel the Rent" activists

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:17.350
may have other reasons for wanting

00:14:17.350 --> 00:14:19.070
to make eviction bans permanent,

00:14:19.070 --> 00:14:20.280
but they've seized on the results

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:22.400
from these studies to pressure lawmakers

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:23.920
and to push for a large expansion

00:14:23.920 --> 00:14:28.503
of the Federal Government's
role in the housing market.

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:32.220
An eviction crisis is looming.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.330
The moratorium is about to expire.

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:38.020
Millions will be faced with
eviction unless Congress acts.

00:14:38.020 --> 00:14:40.380
I'm hearing numbers as
many as 30 million people

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:41.213
could be affected.

00:14:41.213 --> 00:14:42.160
Is that right?

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:45.510
So that's one number
that's been cited for sure.

00:14:45.510 --> 00:14:46.900
To the extent we're going to involve

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.470
scientific researchers
in policy discussions,

00:14:49.470 --> 00:14:51.080
we should focus on the issues

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:54.100
that have been studied by
many people, validated,

00:14:54.100 --> 00:14:56.730
sharing data, argued back and forth.

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:59.400
These shoddy one-off
studies are just ammunition

00:14:59.400 --> 00:15:03.430
for people who want to put a
link saying "studies prove"

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:07.013
in an otherwise purely
speculative article.

